| Name | Title | Seed role | Email (mock) | |---|---|---|---| | Kate Ulepic | Chief Executive Officer | `tenant_admin` | `kate.ulepic@foundation3dp.com` | | Conor Atkins | Chief Operations Officer | `tenant_user` | `conor.atkins@foundation3dp.com` | | Connor Casey | Chief Financial Officer | `tenant_user` | `connor.casey@foundation3dp.com` | | Will Curley | Chief Technology Officer | `tenant_user` | `will.curley@foundation3dp.com` | **Gaps the deck flags ("In Need Of"):** Legal Advisor, Fundraiser, Technician, Homebuilder. 3. Paul Jackson — collaborating partner / shadow admin **Paul Jackson**, VP Strategic Programs, **Entrepreneurs'' Center** — `pjackson@ecinnovates.com` (real). Seeded as a **`partner_user`** collaborating on Foundation''s TVSF build, **appointed by the company (Kate/CEO) as a shadow admin** — i.e. an external EC mentor given broad, all-stage oversight of the Foundation workspace + the TVSF proposal so he can watch the pipeline and coach the team. Password (demo): `DemoPass123!` (same as the founders). 4. Deck facts (raw, for the library atoms) **Formwork economics (slide 4):** Traditional labor 336 hrs → 25 hrs; labor cost $11,588–$14,826 → $2,549 (set crew); material $8,499–$10,561 (15% waste) → $8,074–$10,033 (5% waste). Total savings ~47% of cost, ~11 days. Basis: 2,100 sq ft single-family home, Ohio.

**Technology (slides 6, 8):** plans downloaded to the printer; concrete pumped into the machine; lays concrete one layer at a time; printing trolley moves laterally + vertically; whole machine travels along runway rails; electrically powered. Uses **common local concrete, not proprietary mortar**; an **external gate controls flow at the nozzle**. Prints wall shape directly, eliminating the build-strip-repair-clean cycle; saves ~311 labor hrs/home; cuts production cost ~47–50%; cuts material cost ~60–65% vs mortar-based printers. **Market (slide 5):** TAM **$410B** (2026 housing market); SAM **$23.7B** (formwork in single-family new builds); SOM **$27.4M**. 500,000 home supply/demand gap; 6% mortgage "lock-in"; new-build "buy-down" strategy; foundation 3D printing reduces overall home cost ~4%. **Competitive (slide 7):** vs Traditional Formwork, Vitruvian, COBOD International across Time, Deployment, Material Accessibility, Project Costs, Scalability, Repeatable Workflow. **Dev stage (slide 9):** TRL 6–7. Customer requirements: rapid deployment, reduced labor, structural reliability, locally sourced materials.

**Timeline (slide 10):** 14-month Gantt — Grants+Funding, Product R&D, Beta Prototype, Design Finalization, Third-Party Performance Validation, First Printer Build. **Commercialization (slide 11):** proof-based adoption; local fragmented buyers; barriers trust/ portability/cost; fits subcontractor workflow. Entry via TVSF + SBIR Phase I; sell to GCs/developers; "printing as a service"; partner with local schools + builders; demos/tours → pilot → repeat jobs. **IP (slide 12):** U.S. Patent **11,273,574** (Scalable Three-Dimensional Printing Apparatus); U.S. Patent **10,307,959 B2** (Concrete Delivery System). **Business model (slide 13):** form a JV; program + prepare the print; **charge per cubic yard of concrete**; ≈$20,000 total price per job; contribution margin ≈**37.5%**.

**Financials (slide 15):** fundraising $200k TVSF + $150k SBIR + $1.8M+ equity; SBIR → R&D; pre-seed; goal: prove the market + sustained value to homebuilders. **Project plan (slides 17–18):** Hiring (Mo 1–2, $10k); R&D w/ **Converge** (Mo 3–4, $190k); Beta prototype (Mo 6–8); Grants+Funding (1–14 mo, $1.0M, TVSF/SBIR/VC); R&D on beta (1–12 mo, $450k); Tests & demos (9–14 mo, $30k); Final build (11–14 mo, $300k). **Budget (slides 19–20):** TVSF $200k — Personnel $40k, Equipment $120k, Supplies $10k, Purchased Services $30k (Converge dev services + software partner; COTS + specialized parts; concrete supplies for prototype tests). 5. TVSF Proposal volume — drafted answers (7 pages; Abstract excluded) **Abstract (public, ¼ page).** Foundation is an Ohio pre-seed venture that 3D-prints the *formwork* for residential concrete foundations. By printing the foundation wall shape directly — with common, locally sourced concrete rather than proprietary mortar — Foundation eliminates the traditional build-strip-repair-clean formwork cycle, cutting foundation formwork labor from ~336 hours to ~25 and total formwork cost by roughly 47% on a typical 2,100 sq ft Ohio home. TVSF funds a beta prototype and third-party performance validation, de-risking a $23.7B U.S. formwork market and advancing Ohio''s housing-affordability and advanced-manufacturing agenda.

**#1 — Market Opportunity (TAM).** The 2026 U.S. housing market represents a **$410B TAM** against a structural **~500,000-home supply/demand gap**; "locked-in" 6% mortgages push builders toward new-build "buy-down" economics where every point of cost removed matters. Foundation targets the **formwork** line item of single-family new builds — a **$23.7B SAM** — with an initial serviceable-obtainable **$27.4M SOM** in its regional beachhead. Because foundation 3D printing lowers total home cost ~4% while removing the industry''s most labor-intensive, waste-prone step, it converts a cost center into a competitive advantage for builders operating on thin margins. **#2 — Overview of the Technology.** Foundation''s system prints a foundation''s wall geometry directly: a build plan is downloaded to the printer, concrete is pumped into the machine, and the nozzle lays concrete one layer at a time. A printing trolley moves laterally and vertically while the whole machine travels along runway rails; the system is electrically powered. Two differentiators define it: (a) it uses **common, locally sourced concrete**, not an expensive proprietary mortar; and (b) an **external gate controls material flow at the nozzle**, enabling clean starts/stops and consistent layers. The net effect eliminates the build-strip-repair-clean cycle, saving ~311 labor hours per home and cutting formwork production cost ~47–50% and material cost ~60–65% versus mortar-based concrete printers. **#3 — Development Stage.** The technology is at **TRL 6–7**: core subsystems (delivery, nozzle gate, gantry motion) are demonstrated and the team is building a **beta prototype** for a relevant application environment. MVP customer requirements are explicit — rapid deployment, reduced labor, structural reliability, and use of locally sourced materials. TVSF''s role is to fund the beta prototype build and an independent third-party performance validation, moving the system from demonstrated subsystems (TRL 6–7) toward a field-validated first printer (TRL 7–8). **#4 — Commercialization Strategy (SAM).** Entry is **proof-based**: local, fragmented buyers whose barriers are trust, portability, and cost, and whose workflow already relies on subcontractors. Foundation sells into that workflow as **"printing as a service"** to general contractors and developers, priced per cubic yard of concrete, and partners with **local schools and builders** for demos and workforce pipeline. The motion is **demos/tours → paid pilot → repeat jobs**, using non- dilutive **TVSF + SBIR Phase I** to reach a validated pilot before scaling into the $23.7B formwork SAM.

**#5 — Intellectual Property.** Foundation''s approach is anchored by two issued U.S. patents: **11,273,574** ("Scalable Three-Dimensional Printing Apparatus") covering the scalable gantry/print architecture, and **10,307,959 B2** ("Concrete Delivery System") covering the concrete delivery and nozzle-control mechanism. Together they protect the two core differentiators — the rail-scalable printer and the externally gated, local-concrete delivery path. Freedom-to-operate and continuation strategy around the nozzle-gate control are part of the funded work. **#6 — Business Model & Pro-forma P&L (SOM / economic sense).** Foundation forms a **joint venture** per project, programs and prepares the print, and **charges per cubic yard of concrete placed**, at ≈$20,000 total price per job with a contribution margin of ≈**37.5%**. Printing-as-a-service keeps capital with Foundation and lowers the builder''s barrier to adoption. Against a $27.4M near-term SOM, a modest fleet of printers running repeat jobs reaches sustainable unit economics quickly; the pro-forma (in $1,000s) scales job volume as pilots convert to repeat contracts, with gross margin expanding as printer utilization rises. **#7 — Current Financial Stage & Fundraising.** Foundation is **pre-seed**. The capital plan pairs **$200k TVSF** and **$150k SBIR Phase I** (non-dilutive, directed at R&D and the beta prototype) with a targeted **$1.8M+ equity** round. Equity investment is critical to scaling beyond the first printer; the non-dilutive grants exist to **prove the market and de-risk the technology first**, so the equity raise is priced on validated performance rather than on a concept. TVSF is the near-term unlock for the validated beta prototype that makes the round investable. **#8 — Team / Management.** **Kate Ulepic (CEO)** leads strategy and fundraising; **Conor Atkins (COO)** owns operations and print execution; **Connor Casey (CFO)** owns finance and the capital plan; **Will Curley (CTO)** owns the printer and delivery technology. The team has identified and is actively recruiting for its known gaps — legal advisor, fundraiser, technician, and homebuilder partner — and is mentored through the **Entrepreneurs'' Center** (Paul Jackson, VP Strategic Programs).

**#9 — Competitive Landscape.** Against **Traditional Formwork**, **Vitruvian**, and **COBOD International**, Foundation wins on the dimensions that decide adoption: | Dimension | Foundation | Traditional Formwork | Vitruvian | COBOD | |---|:--:|:--:|:--:|:--:| | Time (days) | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | | Deployment | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | | Material accessibility | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | | Project costs | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | | Scalability | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | | Repeatable workflow | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | Foundation is the only option that combines **local-material accessibility** and **lowest project cost** with rapid, scalable, repeatable deployment — traditional formwork is slow and labor-heavy, while other printers depend on costly proprietary mortar. **#10 — Economic Impact (Ohio).** Foundation is Ohio-based and Ohio-building. It lowers the cost of new single-family homes in a state facing a supply gap, supports builder margins, and creates skilled advanced-manufacturing and print-operator jobs. By partnering with **local schools and builders** for demos and training, it seeds a regional workforce pipeline and keeps concrete spend with **local** suppliers rather than proprietary-mortar vendors — compounding the in-state economic return of each job.

**#11 — Project Plan.** Milestones (name · description · success criteria · timeframe · approx. funds): **Hire core team** — fill technician/operator roles — team in place — Months 1–2 — **$10,000**. **R&D with Converge** — engineer the beta printer + delivery/gate — validated subsystem specs — Months 3–4 — **$190,000**. **Beta prototype build** — assemble the first field-representative printer — prints a test wall to spec — Months 6–8 — (within R&D budget). **Third-party performance validation** — independent structural + throughput test — passing validation report — Months 9–14 — **~$30,000**.

**First printer build** — production-representative unit for pilots — pilot-ready printer — Months 11–14 — **~$300,000 (blended grant/VC)**. 6. TVSF Budget volume — $200,000 (OTF Project Funds) | Spend type | OTF Project Funds | Notes | |---|--:|---| | Personnel | $40,000 | founding-team time + first technical hires against the beta build | | Equipment | $120,000 | COTS + specialized printer/delivery parts for the beta prototype | | Supplies | $10,000 | concrete + consumables to run prototype print tests | | Purchased Services | $30,000 | Converge development services + a software-development partner | | **Total** | **$200,000** | | Narrative: the bulk of the grant funds **Equipment** (COTS + specialized parts to build the beta prototype with Converge) and **Purchased Services** (Converge engineering + software partner); **Supplies** cover concrete for prototype testing; **Personnel** covers founding-team time and the first technical hire. 7. Foundation spotlight buckets (interest areas → for ranking opportunities) **Additive Construction & 3D Printing** (primary) **Advanced Manufacturing / Automation** **Construction Technology & Housing Affordability** **Materials — Concrete & Low-Carbon Cement** **Non-dilutive Capital (SBIR/STTR + State)** 8. SBIR/STTR opportunities to rank against Foundation''s buckets (loaded as additional master opps so Paul sees ranked SBIRs next to the TVSF build)

**Army ERDC — Additive Construction of Expeditionary Structures (ACES)** — 3D-printed concrete structures for basing. *Very high fit* (additive construction + concrete). **NSF SBIR Phase I — Advanced Manufacturing: Construction Automation & Robotics.** *High fit.* **DOE SBIR — Low-Carbon Concrete & Cement Materials.** *Medium-high fit* (materials bucket). **NASA SBIR — Additive Construction for Off-World Habitats.** *Medium fit* (adjacent additive construction, non-terrestrial). **NSF STTR — Robotics for the Built Environment.** *Medium fit* (automation, university partner).', '[{"id":"514459b0-f574-4e27-878f-19de65e8f456","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"Foundation — company profile & TVSF content (digested from `3DCP_Final_Prez_1.pptx`)","level":1},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-10T13:20:08.195Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-10T13:20:08.194Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"2ebd77fb-33d4-484d-9aac-c09988b1cfbd","type":"text_block","style":{},"content":{"text":"> Source: the Entrepreneurs'' Center team''s final presentation (26 slides), forwarded by > **Paul Jackson, VP Strategic Programs, Entrepreneurs'' Center** (`pjackson@ecinnovates.com`). > This file is the **source of truth** for the Foundation seed: it drives the tenant, the > founder accounts, the library atoms, and the drafted TVSF proposal (12 questions + budget). > Structure/facts are transcribed from the deck; narrative is written fresh for the proposal."},"history":[{"action":"created","actor_id":"system:import","timestamp":"2026-08-10T13:20:08.195Z","actor_name":"Document Import"}],"provenance":{"source":"imported","drafted_at":"2026-08-10T13:20:08.195Z","drafted_by":"system:import"},"library_eligible":true},{"id":"b33d323a-5946-415d-abb1-6fd0f709bdf6","type":"heading","style":{},"content":{"text":"1. Company snapshot","level":2},"history":[{"action":"